WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.099 --> 00:00:05.120
Imagine you're in a game show with three doors.
One has a car, and the others have goats.

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:10.730
You pick a door, say #1, and the host reveals
a goat behind another door, #3. They ask if

00:00:10.730 --> 00:00:12.769
you want to switch your choice to door #2.

00:00:12.769 --> 00:00:14.610
So, do you switch?

00:00:14.610 --> 00:00:17.699
Most people think it doesn’t matter whether
you switch or not.

00:00:17.699 --> 00:00:20.630
But switching increases your chances of winning.

00:00:20.630 --> 00:00:24.960
Initially, your chosen door has a ⅓ chance
of winning, while the other doors together

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:31.080
have a ⅔ chance. The host reveals a losing
door, so they’re filtering out the bad choice,

00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:33.960
leaving the other door with a ⅔ chance of
winning.

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:37.430
It’s easier to understand if there are 100
doors.

00:00:37.430 --> 00:00:43.210
Your initial choice has a 1/100 chance of
winning, while the other doors have a 99/100

00:00:43.210 --> 00:00:44.210
chance.

00:00:44.210 --> 00:00:48.980
The host then filters out the options for
you by eliminating 98 bad doors they know

00:00:48.980 --> 00:00:50.550
don't have the prize.

00:00:50.550 --> 00:00:56.410
This leaves the remaining door with the rest
of the odds, a 99/100 chance of having the

00:00:56.410 --> 00:00:57.120
prize.

